#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134851} :activity/id #uuid "cc83e826-b428-5261-8109-f36f4450dc4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051000 :topic/id #uuid "789238e2-4251-5080-bbc3-e713bd84a64b" :topic/math-academy-id 2109} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135122 :tutorial/content "Until now, we've been dealing with line integrals with respect to arc length. In this lesson, we will learn about other types of line integral. \n\nSuppose that $C$ is a path in the $xy$-plane with initial point $A$ and terminal point $B.$ The line integral of the function $f(x,y)$ **with respect to $\\boldsymbol x$** along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo understand what this integral represents, we imagine traversing $C$ from $A$ to $B.$ At each point $P$ that lies on $C,$ we project the point on $f$ that lies directly above $P$ onto the plane $y=0.$ Doing this for every point on $C$ traces out a curve. The line integral of $f$ with respect to $x$ gives the (signed) area bounded by this curve and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/62/62c07a36cbf79459021ee7be125536a8f9c1bc3c193a99eabfcd3fc851b8eb0c.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $x,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f1254b7-43b2-5b19-b5e9-cf33ff6cffe8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5812 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a502006-31bd-5b97-b374-0fc7fe27b6e6" :step/math-academy-id 35614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :question/id #uuid "bbd835f9-a428-558c-9db7-838d02267a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530659} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530652}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"} :step/id #uuid "836166ec-b6a4-5203-a460-a9fbed3d6962" :step/math-academy-id 35615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133708 :question/id #uuid "7417f4d8-262c-5d16-9b35-f1c9710345e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9513"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527925}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "3c7d4502-6f95-54db-ae3c-2f1adac21e09" :step/math-academy-id 35616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527980}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "10fbb7b7-ba3c-5cff-a825-8a6c4e82bc79" :step/math-academy-id 35642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135123 :tutorial/content "The **line integral with respect to** $\\boldsymbol y$ of the function $f(x,y)$ along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nThis integral represents the total area accumulated when $f$ is projected on the plane $x=0$ as $C$ is traversed from the initial point $A$ to the terminal point $B.$ \n\n![](images/9c/9ccbabd17ff18511ed26e8098b2fad41104bbd921a32eb639ae1a74bcab6f94b.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $y,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e58d5cab-69bb-507b-a225-b621efc9b93f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5816 :tutorial/title "Line Integrals With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "452fc7d7-48f9-5478-a20c-0ad745c8bca0" :step/math-academy-id 35670 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134853}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133995}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536952} :activity/id #uuid "b4a753ff-d7f8-5373-9dfa-b6d7d7b72e09" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051000 :topic/id #uuid "789238e2-4251-5080-bbc3-e713bd84a64b" :topic/math-academy-id 2109} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021c19b6-a87f-59bb-986b-654c56b918a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256802} {:answer/id #uuid "54fdfd50-de07-5cfb-b6d6-270b66947185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257507} {:answer/id #uuid "6abe279c-1db9-56ef-99fe-6e272def1150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} {:answer/id #uuid "debdbff0-5139-5a2f-ac8e-84956824c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258722} {:answer/id #uuid "f66ec332-aeec-5f7f-8bb0-93630c7f3749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} :answer-field/id #uuid "4358961a-328e-53e0-ad60-06b7463ba396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd888ac9-9398-5cd7-abd9-7da37aaa7d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-124175" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(\\sqrt{x-1}+y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23b5296c-9050-57da-ab12-b54ca52a68dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091b6b7e-813c-5b73-865c-f669e169c171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-6t+9)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256862} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2d80f4-0dad-59e0-a83c-87ee7e93fd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257567} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb0bf06-323d-5f15-8bf3-af2b48419f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-6t+9)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258045} {:answer/id #uuid "c51659c0-3351-506b-83b7-da83cb4dac4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+6t+9)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258505} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3799de-5093-52dd-b475-702c04f8913f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257567} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc17d7b-1584-5ada-831d-f64a488b2cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9417c70-141e-5010-bf56-db9beb8a2e51" :question/math-academy-id "q-124121" :question/problem "![](images/36/36b985c811eb28d8220017c6b7407b3a104554d785dafdb35f17553c262bdc35.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-2)$ to the point $(2,4),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,6t-2⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(2,4).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(-2+t(4-(-2)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,6t-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)\\cdot (6t-2) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(18{t}^{2}-12t+2)\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67e62851-cb57-5f03-806b-78bd3a0fae63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536913}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf68642-1bc2-5ac7-9a05-766d932162e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387500} {:answer/id #uuid "f72d462d-8b4a-5ce9-af5e-b55e5d4bd6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387501} {:answer/id #uuid "64c3b71b-6781-591c-9b16-935e82c7d8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3){sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387502} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3ffd3c-9264-5a32-8db9-c3420cd55260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387503} {:answer/id #uuid "38807c4a-be0d-5f98-b548-41b11aa72f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3){cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387503} :answer-field/id #uuid "164502c8-6757-5ba5-b73c-b45e5adc8b09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d9bcf07-4605-5aed-9b42-699d630fbd7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124298" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1956c8291f1bcecd7d37082110861ed7265fa40f7f85cbc69221cfdc5bea2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =12{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12 \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8338d321-9ae2-5f16-a804-d4198a730ec4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5c27e6-6d15-5543-baca-509a958d1428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369253} {:answer/id #uuid "81b4c97c-6573-5711-9cd1-f51a4a264e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369254} {:answer/id #uuid "4b80e13c-c214-5a76-9bff-a55badcb706d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369255} {:answer/id #uuid "938debfc-b447-5b7c-a524-0c933d4da736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369256} {:answer/id #uuid "977e7ec8-5c46-57e8-b421-0c2f023be513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369257} :answer-field/id #uuid "29184995-008b-5dbe-af80-c01d22170858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bdf2c9bf-2367-5618-ad0e-1a35e799a6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124120" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f531f2467284ef84e0af0758a8e8554f1e5f72ea08d30d0b80c83076fbe5d7a3.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,0)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(0+t(1-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & =1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8bd15231-6077-5acd-b210-63ea5dfd7b32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a01ac03-516d-5191-b62a-df55277746ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369280} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2ea435-9b78-5237-b1de-d27c14d67721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369281} {:answer/id #uuid "340e5763-cb2f-5d3a-ab41-9e4aab2f5062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369282} {:answer/id #uuid "12a066d8-58b4-511c-9cb2-861f5afc7e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369283} {:answer/id #uuid "99c2fa33-7e84-5fa9-b445-20cdce34028b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369281} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a352f7-9105-5249-b023-dd67c620d14d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1880828-25c5-5042-afdb-e835de5c511c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124283" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f83043b44a8ad9181e66de57357397deb49c4b0e79b5e6e16330305a58391148.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(4,0)$ to the point $(-4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de912477-5c57-5e4d-865f-02d49e044bb5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536945}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536281}]}
